    "created_at": "2020-10-29T21:41:06Z",
    "updated_at": "2020-10-29T21:41:06Z",
    "body": "> Is the notion here: \"_If a navigation to an application protocol would result in a prompt, fail it unless there's a permission delegation?_\" or is it \"_**Any** navigation to an application protocol should fail it unless there's a permission delegation?_\"\r\n\r\nAh, that’s a great question. I think it should be the former. Browsers should still be able to maintain a list of protocols that will not get this treatment.\r\n\r\n> In either case, would this change be limited to all such navigations? E.g. Would it apply only if the subframe tried to navigate itself or a descendent, or would the prompt also be shown if the navigation target were the top-level frame, or a _blank tab?\r\n\r\nAlso a good question. It should probably cover all cases where the subframe triggers the navigation, including top-level targets, to be effective.\r\n\r\nI guess realistically we can’t prevent an iframe from opening a new tab which will then forward to a protocol address on its own, though at least it will need user interaction for that, so that is the same as with other permissions.\r\n\r\n> I think we'd need to look at metrics to determine how breaking a change like this would be.\r\n> \r\n> I'd be especially worried about popular protocols handled by external applications like \"mailto:\".\r\n\r\nYes, again, I think we would exclude mailto and maybe others (there’s a list in Firefox IIUC) and @Trikolon is currently adding Telemetry for measuring protocol handler navigations in Firefox.\r\n\r\nAlso, as I mentioned, in Firefox we could imagine not immediately blocking protocol navigation but rather starting out by prompting without a checkbox and maybe having a roadmap for eventual blocking. It does depend on the numbers, as you said.",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
